# Git workflow (main by default)

Stay on **`main`**. Do not create a topic branch, extra git worktree, or `wip/<topic>` unless the user explicitly asks **or** a spec document requires isolation.

**Before touching any code:** if the tree has committable work, make a **fallback commit** on `main` first (restore point). If the tree is already clean, HEAD is the fallback — do not invent an empty commit. Then edit.

After a **named research step** lands and tests pass, commit on `main`. Do not wait for “please commit”. Never `git push` unless the user asks.

A step is: a new strategy version registered; experiment A/B/C (or similar) implemented and run; a selector/grid closed as “didn’t work”; a baseline set locked. Not a step: PARAM_GRID tweaks mid-grid, analysis-only chat, a spotted bug you have not been told to fix, failing tests.

Do not rewrite `main` history. Do not commit `data/runs/`, secrets, or one-off analysis blobs.

Commit message: 1–2 sentences on why (what the step decided), same HEREDOC style as the repo. Run `git status` / `git diff` / `git log` first. Leave the tree committable; say that you committed and on which branch.
